{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"node-events-streams","title":"EventEmitter & Streams","group":"Browser & Node.js","order":59,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Most of Node is built on two ideas: objects that emit named events, and streams that move data in chunks instead of loading everything into memory.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-broadcast\"></i>EventEmitter</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>import { EventEmitter, once } from \"node:events\";\n\nclass Order extends EventEmitter {\n  place(item) {\n    this.emit(\"placed\", { item, at: \"12:00\" });\n    if (item === \"unknown\") this.emit(\"error\", new Error(\"no such item\"));\n  }\n}\nconst order = new Order();\norder.on(\"placed\", ({ item }) =&gt; console.log(\"email: placed\", item));\norder.once(\"placed\", () =&gt; console.log(\"analytics: first order only\"));\norder.on(\"error\", (err) =&gt; console.log(\"handled error:\", err.message));\n\norder.place(\"book\");\norder.place(\"unknown\");\nconsole.log(order.listenerCount(\"placed\"), order.eventNames());\n\nsetTimeout(() =&gt; order.emit(\"shipped\", \"TRACK-1\"), 5);\nconst [tracking] = await once(order, \"shipped\");      // promise-based wait\nconsole.log(\"shipped with\", tracking);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>email: placed book\nanalytics: first order only\nemail: placed unknown\nhandled error: no such item\n1 [ 'placed', 'error' ]\nshipped with TRACK-1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Emitting <code>'error'</code> with no <code>'error'</code> listener <strong>throws</strong> and crashes the process. Listeners run <strong>synchronously</strong>, in registration order, inside <code>emit</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-right\"></i>Streams</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Readable</td><td><code>fs.createReadStream</code>, HTTP request (server side), <code>process.stdin</code></td></tr><tr><td>Writable</td><td><code>fs.createWriteStream</code>, HTTP response, <code>process.stdout</code></td></tr><tr><td>Duplex</td><td>TCP socket</td></tr><tr><td>Transform</td><td><code>zlib.createGzip()</code>, encryption, parsers</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>import { Readable, Transform, Writable } from \"node:stream\";\nimport { pipeline } from \"node:stream/promises\";\n\nconst source = Readable.from([\"alpha\\n\", \"beta\\n\", \"gamma\\n\"]);\nconst upper = new Transform({\n  transform(chunk, _enc, callback) {\n    callback(null, chunk.toString().toUpperCase());\n  },\n});\nconst collected = [];\nconst sink = new Writable({\n  write(chunk, _enc, callback) {\n    collected.push(chunk.toString().trim());\n    callback();\n  },\n});\nawait pipeline(source, upper, sink);     // handles back-pressure and errors\nconsole.log(collected);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ 'ALPHA', 'BETA', 'GAMMA' ]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><strong>Back-pressure</strong>: when the consumer is slower than the producer, <code>pipeline</code>/<code>pipe</code> pause the source so memory stays bounded — the reason to stream a 10 GB file instead of <code>readFile</code>-ing it.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why use streams instead of reading a whole file?</summary><div><p>Streams process data chunk by chunk, so memory use stays constant regardless of size, work starts before all data arrives, and back-pressure keeps fast producers from overwhelming slow consumers.</p></div></details></section>"}